Abstract
This paper presents the design and functional verification of a novel 3D-stacked hardware security logic lock architecture, named 3D Chip Security Logic Lock. The design implements a multi-step physical intercept and audit mechanism using three vertically stacked layers. The system comprises four core modules: (1)
forksheet_core
(Engine Layer): ARX-transform-based calculation flow processor, forcing data upward to the audit layer via vertical VIA logic leap; (2)
m2_intercept
(M2 Audit Layer): PGU logic array executing Five Axioms Check, covering Bio-Hash match, Phys-XOR zero verification, entropy check, sampling integrity, and truth gate consistency; (3)
sovereign_base
(Brake Layer): upon receiving audit failure, sequentially executes back-gate bias adjustment (nanosecond-level), logic gate deactivation, and permanent hard-wired shutdown; (4)
tsv_channel
(Vertical VIA Channel): handshake routing protocol between three layers. All modules were verified using Icarus Verilog 12.0 on EDA Playground with EPWave waveform confirmation. 35 test cases, 100% pass rate, zero latch inferences, zero timing violations.
Keywords:
3D chip security; physical intercept; hardware logic lock; back-gate bias; PUF; Five Axioms; RTL verification; nanosecond response; hard-wired shutdown; stacked chip security
1. Introduction
Escalating chip supply chain security threats — hardware Trojans, device counterfeiting, and reverse engineering — demand security mechanisms operating at the physical layer, beyond what logic-level defenses can provide. In 3D-stacked IC architectures, the physical separation between silicon dies enables a fundamentally new security dimension: inter-layer physical interception. By controlling data flow through Through-Silicon Via (TSV) channels, deploying non-destructive physical audit at the M2 metal layer, and implementing nanosecond-level hardware forced shutdown in a dedicated brake die, a "physical logic lock" can be realized that no software attack can bypass.

This paper presents the 3D Chip Security Logic Lock: a complete RTL implementation of this concept, verified using Icarus Verilog 12.0 with EPWave waveform confirmation. The design targets high-security applications including military, financial, and industrial control systems.

