3. Core Module Design
3.1 forksheet_core — Engine Layer
The engine layer processes input data through ARX (Add-Rotate-XOR) transformation, controlled by a 6-state FSM (IDLE → FETCH → PROCESS → LEAP → TRANSMIT → WAIT_ACK). The LEAP state asserts the logic_leap signal, triggering upward data transmission through the vertical VIA channel. Four ARX rounds ensure adequate diffusion, confirmed by avalanche effect testing (different inputs produce completely different outputs).

3.2 m2_intercept — M2 Audit Layer
The audit layer implements the Five Axioms Check, providing multi-dimensional security verification:

Axiom 1 (Bio-Hash Match): Uploaded data must exactly match the stored Bio-Hash reference. Axiom 2 (Phys-XOR Zero): XOR of data with Phys-XOR baseline must equal zero. Axiom 3 (Entropy Check): 64-bit Hamming weight must fall within [16, 48], preventing degenerate all-zero or all-ones conditions. Axiom 4 (Sampling Integrity): Non-destructive sampling process integrity confirmation. Axiom 5 (Truth Gate Consistency): Parity relationship verification of selected bits.

Failure of any single axiom triggers FAIL verdict and activates the brake layer.

3.3 sovereign_base — Brake Layer
Upon receiving the FAIL signal, the brake layer executes a three-step forced shutdown sequence: Step 3.1: Back-Gate Bias Adjustment — incrementally ramps back-gate bias voltage to maximum (15 levels), corresponding to nanosecond-level transistor state modification; Step 3.2: Logic Gate Deactivation — disables gate drive signals; Step 3.3: Hard-wired Permanent Shutdown — enters HARDLOCK state, releasable only by hardware rst_n, immune to any software intervention.

3.4 tsv_channel — Vertical VIA Routing
The TSV channel implements a 4-state handshake protocol (IDLE → UP → DOWN → DONE) routing data upward from the engine to the audit layer and result signals downward from the audit to the brake layer. Ensures atomic, race-free inter-layer data transfer.

